---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Antique, an independent journal and personal publication — its author, its intentions, and its colophon."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the publication</p>
      <h1 class="page-head__title">A small press of one.</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede">
        Antique is written and edited by {SITE.author.name}. It exists to make room for a certain
        kind of attention — unhurried, unoptimised, and mostly offline.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <div class="about-grid">
        <aside class="about-aside">
          <div class="about-aside__portrait">
            <img src="/logo.svg" alt="The Antique antler emblem" width="120" height="120" />
          </div>
          <p class="about-aside__caption">
            The antler, drawn by hand.<br />
            A mark for the half-said and the half-remembered.
          </p>
          <div class="site-footer__links">
            <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
            <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I started Antique because I wanted somewhere to put the long thoughts — the ones that
            do not fit into a message, a thread, or a caption. It is a personal journal in the old
            sense: a place to think in public, slowly, and to keep a record of the thinking.
          </p>
          <p>
            The name is not about age or objects. It is about the quality of attention we give to
            things that have been handled a great deal: words, memories, ordinary days. An antique
            is valuable because someone kept it. This is a place for keeping.
          </p>

          <h2>What you will find</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Essays</strong> are the centre of the publication: finished pieces, usually
            between one and four thousand words, on attention, memory, technology, solitude, and the
            textures of a working life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Notes</strong> are the margins. Short observations, quotations, and fragments
            that have not yet become anything else. They are published without polish, because
            polish is often what kills them.
          </p>

          <h2>What this is not</h2>
          <p>
            There is no content calendar. There are no sponsors, growth targets, or engagement
            metrics. Nothing here is generated in order to be consumed quickly, and nothing is
            written to be agreed with. If a piece is wrong, it will be revised in public and the
            revision dated.
          </p>

          <h2>Correspondence</h2>
          <p>
            Letters are welcome and read carefully. Write to
            <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. I answer what I can, when I can.
          </p>

          <h2>Colophon</h2>
          <p>
            Set in <em>Cormorant Garamond</em> for display and body, <em>Inter</em> for interface
            text, and <em>JetBrains Mono</em> for the small labels that keep things in order. Built
            with Astro, hosted as static files, indexed by Pagefind. The paper is a colour, not a
            texture. The antler was drawn with a single line and then corrected many times.
          </p>
          <p>
            All writing is © {new Date().getFullYear()} {SITE.author.name}. Please quote with
            attribution and a link; do not republish in full without asking.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <Newsletter
        title="Letters, not broadcasts."
        copy="If you would like the occasional essay in your inbox — and nothing else — leave an address below."
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
